1.5 Stress
In the absence of any volumetric (e.g. gravitational or electromagnetic) effects, a force can only be transmitted to a solid by being applied to its boundary. It is, therefore, natural to consider the force per unit area or stress applied at that boundary. To do so, we now analyse an infinitesimal surface element, whose area and unit normal are da and n respectively. If it is contained within a stressed medium, then the material on (say) the side into which n points will exert a force df on the element. (By Newton’s third law, the material on the other side will also exert a force equal to −df .) In the expectation that the force should be proportional to the area da, we write
df = σ da, (1.5.1)
